      detail: "The name \"watermelon\" first appears in accounts left by Central Plains people who returned north from the Khitan in the Five Dynasties; from the Song it was widely grown in the Central Plains, and the chapters on melon in the old books grow thicker and thicker. Works such as the Southern Song's Songmo jiwen go further, recording the threads of how the melon spread south. An earlier clue lies in the Record of the Slain Barbarians by Hu Qiao of the Five Dynasties: he spent seven years travelling north with the Khitan, saw the locals begin to eat melon there, and wrote down its origin as \"the Khitan broke the Uyghurs and obtained this kind.\" From this later readers inferred that the melon came by turns through the Uyghurs and the Khitan before reaching the Central Plains. From Song and Yuan times the melon fields widened, and in Jiangnan villages there was melon at every door. Fan Chengda's \"green vines lying on soft sand through the frost — in these years, melon eaten everywhere\" writes the village scene; Wen Tianxiang's \"Ode to the Melon\" — \"I draw the golden-hilted blade, I cleave the jade-green jar\" — renders its crispness. A history of the melon is folded into these gazetteers and these lines of verse."
