      detail: "Melon sauce (guadojiang) is a traditional pickle of Central Plains households: the juice of the melon is mixed with cooked soybeans dusted with qu, set in an earthenware basin to ferment in the sun, and only after a full stretch of dog days does the sauce smell right — it is what goes into the flatbread and the mixed noodles. The craft of making melon sauce looks simple, and patience is in every part of it: soybeans are boiled through and dusted with flour, covered until a golden mould grows, then go into the vat with melon flesh and juice, Sichuan pepper and ginger slices, and the whole is veiled in gauze to sun. It is stirred once in the morning and once in the evening, and after a full stretch of dog days the sauce is red and bright and richly fragrant — the household that makes it keeps watch over that vat all summer long. Carving melon lanterns is another kind of cleverness: take a thick-skinned melon, scrape the red flesh clean, and cut flowers, birds and beasts into the green rind; light a candle inside at night and the green skin glows warm, and the children carry it round the yard. One vat of sauce feeds a table for a year; one lantern lights a summer. Both are the thought that an ordinary melon turns into."
