      detail: "Melon in the hot days clears heat and quenches thirst, full of water — the plainest hydrating fruit of summer. Traditional food culture holds the melon to be cooling in nature: it clears heat and relieves summer heat, and when the middle stretch of summer leaves the mouth dry, a slice or two settles the burning at once. Folk even joke that \"half a melon in the hot days means no need for a doctor's prescription.\" There is a measure in how to eat it: chilled is a treat, but for those with sensitive stomachs, merely cool is better; for diabetics, taste a little only; cut melon should go back into the fridge promptly, and melon left overnight at room temperature should no longer be eaten raw. Peeled of its outer green coat, the rind can also be dressed cold — one melon, two flavours. The older generation wasted nothing: one watermelon, sweet from end to end."
