      text: "When the greenhouse strawberries turn red in winter and early spring, the picking farms on the edge of the city fill with parents and children, and picking strawberries has become a new generation's ritual of tasting what is newly in season.",
      detail: "When the greenhouse strawberries turn red in winter and early spring, the picking farms on the edge of the city fill with parents and children, and picking strawberries has become a new generation's ritual of tasting what is newly in season. The older generation's strawberry memories begin much earlier: a small bed in front of and behind the house, flowering in spring, reddening by late spring, children squatting at the edge of it keeping watch, picking one red berry at a time. Most never reached the fruit bowl — they went into the stomach on the way. Now the greenhouse has pulled the season forward into winter, and strawberries sit right beside the Spring Festival and the Lantern Festival; in the fruit baskets carried on visits, a box of red berries is both festive and presentable. There are rules at the picking farms too: twist the ripe berries off by their stem, leave the green ones alone, and eat them on the spot when they are sweetest. From a small bed at the front of the house to row upon row under plastic, the strawberry has carried the idea of \"tasting the new\" all the way from late spring to the turn of the year."
