      detail: "The strawberry is delicate, and the moment it comes off the plant something has to be done with it. The classic answer is jam: hull and halve the berries, add sugar and a little lemon juice, let them sit until they give off their gel, then boil gently until the mixture coats a spoon, bottle and chill. It is spring on bread and stirred into yoghurt. To keep it longer, dry it instead, baking slowly at a low temperature until it is supple and no longer sticky. There is craft in preserving them too: whole berries layered with sugar, the syrup that draws out makes a strawberry drink when diluted with water; others soak them in rice vinegar or spirits to make strawberry vinegar and strawberry wine, sealed for a few months and still fragrant when opened. The one thing jam cannot stand is a high flame — a scorched bottom turns the whole pot bitter. Low heat and patience are, for the strawberry, very nearly the only way to keep it."
