      detail: "The commonest thing said in the strawberry fields is \"pick only the ripe ones\" — one red berry at a time, the green ones left for another day. The weekend picking farm is like a class in session: the owner sets the rules first, when she hands out the little baskets — walk along the rows, do not tread on the beds; pick only what is fully red, pinch the stem gently, do not pull. The children squat at the edge of the rows picking and choosing with more patience than the grown-ups, and whoever finds a heart-shaped berry is happy for the afternoon. Some farms keep \"their own row\" for regulars, who come in both spring and winter seasons and are treated like old neighbours. At the weighing, the owner drops two more berries into the basket for the children to eat on the way, and a \"come back when the flowers open again\" turns the promise of spring into something that feels like visiting relatives. What a basket of strawberries buys is not only sweetness, but the chance for a child to learn to wait."
