      detail: "The strawberry is a herb, and what it needs managing is the rhythm of one crop a year. It is planted at the end of autumn, on raised beds covered with film — the film keeps the warmth in, keeps weeds down, and keeps the fruit off the soil. In early spring come thinning of flowers and fruit, and the first flush of flowers is often pinched off so the nutrition goes to what comes after. The runners must be pinched back often, or they run everywhere and the fruit comes out small. Grey mould is the strawberry's old adversary, and ventilation, lowered humidity and the prompt removal of diseased leaves and fruit matter most; water at the root, not over the leaf. Picking has to be in the early morning, once the dew has dried, when the fruit has turned about eighty per cent red and the calyx is still firm; cut with a short stem left on, and hold the berry by its waist rather than its face. Line the baskets with a single layer only — one berry pressed on another bruises the look, and whether this basket of strawberries sells for a good price turns entirely on that one step."
