      detail: "The strawberry's character lies in eating it fresh: wash, drain, eat — and its vitamin C content ranks well among fruits. Traditional food culture treats the strawberry as a small fruit of spring, clearing and moistening: sweet-sour, full of water, and a few after a meal to cut the richness. Such sayings are pleasant to hear, but there is no need to carry the burden of their claimed powers. Washing takes a little care: hull the berries just before eating and rub the whole berry gently under running water, because hulling them before soaking lets dirty water soak back into the flesh. Adults should check the fruit before the children eat it, and anything with a broken surface should be left out. Cream, condensed milk and jam are all additions, and their sweetness counts against the day's total; fresh and in season is the best value, and once the season has passed, a small jar of homemade jam will hold the craving off for a while."
