      detail: "Through the homophony of \"ping\" (apple) and \"ping\" (peace), the apple became the fruit of peace carried to those who are ill and given at New Year; parents also slip one into a child's exam bag for the luck of it. On the Mid-Autumn fruit plate, red apples stand alongside mooncakes and pomegranates, carrying the auspicious weight of the reunion table. In the growing districts, the autumn harvest is marked by an opening celebration — fruit festivals, a king-of-the-fruit contest, crates of red fruit loaded onto carts, so that the harvest itself becomes a festival. If some household's fruit takes first prize, the whole village shares the honour. The young have begun packing peace apples into gift boxes to hand to classmates and friends at Christmas Eve, and the old fruit custom has taken a new graft in the hands of a new generation. One apple has been telling the word \"peace\" for a hundred years and more, and each telling grows more lively — and more particular about how it must be done."
