      text: "Fruit shears, bamboo baskets and compost for the soil are the old three of the apple orchard; dwarfed close planting is the new way, clearing the orchard and whitewashing the trunks the old rule. Old and new used together give a stable crop.",
      detail: "Fruit shears, bamboo baskets and compost for the soil are the old three of the apple orchard; dwarfed close planting is the new way, clearing the orchard and whitewashing the trunks the old rule. Old and new used together give a stable crop. The shears are wiped clean and oiled after use, the cuts come away clean, and only then does a branch wound close quickly. The picking bag hangs slung at the waist, and when it is full it is emptied into a basket; no basket is held overnight, because fruit pressed through the night wilts. Composting to improve the soil is the root of the old method: leaves and straw, rotted through, go back into the orchard, and once the soil is loose the roots go deep; only with rich soil is the fruit sweet. Whitewashing the trunks uses lime mixed to a wash, brushed on once before winter, guarding against frost and insects, so that a row of them looks like a row of winter coats. With the old three and the new ways used side by side, the orchard gets better every year — the tools fit the hand, and the work halves."
