      detail: "Apples in autumn help the spleen and open the appetite, and eating them with the skin gives more fibre — washing is the precondition, and wax and farm residues come off with a rub under running water. Traditional food culture holds the apple to be neutral in nature; the old saying about helping the spleen in autumn has been passed down for generations, it suits young and old alike, and one apple after a meal is just right for cutting the richness. For children with weak digestion, the grandmother at home steams an apple until soft and easy to eat, with a little sugar it becomes a sweet. Dried apple and jam concentrate the sugar, so the portion goes down accordingly. Cut and soaked in light salt water, the flesh does not go brown; the apple keeps well, and left in a cool place it is good for ten days or a fortnight. \"An apple a day\" is a good slogan, but a balanced diet is the root of it — no single fruit should be treated as a cure-all."
